40:14.540 --> 40:16.480
What exactly did they build in this repository?

40:16.760 --> 40:24.580
They built a formal verification artifact using a highly respected proof assistant called Lean 4.9 0.0.

40:24.580 --> 40:31.900
Specifically, they created a frozen mathematical specimen named admissibilitytransitionsystem.lean.

40:32.200 --> 40:33.120
Why does it need to be frozen?

40:33.400 --> 40:38.400
Because in strict scientific testing, your control group has to be perfectly, unalterably stable.

40:38.520 --> 40:38.740
Okay.

40:38.860 --> 40:39.320
That makes sense.

40:39.480 --> 40:40.880
This specimen is beautifully minimal.

40:41.120 --> 40:42.740
It has absolutely zero dependencies.

40:43.280 --> 40:45.340
There is no external mathlib imported.

40:45.780 --> 40:47.960
It uses just pure Lean core code.

40:48.140 --> 40:49.500
It is completely self-contained.

40:49.500 --> 40:51.240
And what's the structure of it?

40:51.500 --> 40:54.320
Logically, it's designed with an asymmetric dependency graph.

40:55.240 --> 40:56.660
It looks a bit like a Y shape.

40:56.700 --> 40:57.580
A Y shape?

40:57.640 --> 40:57.820
Yeah.

40:57.920 --> 41:03.020
It has two separate logical branches of proofs that happen to share one common dependency right

41:03.020 --> 41:04.840
in the middle, which they call D2.

41:05.020 --> 41:08.080
Why design it like a Y shape with a shared middle node?

41:08.440 --> 41:11.040
To enable highly targeted mutation testing.

41:11.040 --> 41:17.660
If you mutate that shared dependency, D2, you should theoretically see the failure propagate

41:17.660 --> 41:19.340
down both branches of the Y.

41:19.500 --> 41:19.760
Right.

41:19.940 --> 41:24.520
But if you mutate a node higher up on just one branch, the other branch should remain

41:24.520 --> 41:27.280
perfectly verified and completely unaffected.

41:27.340 --> 41:27.600
Ah.

41:27.760 --> 41:31.980
It's a way to prove that the type dependency edges actually track the blast radius of a

41:31.980 --> 41:33.760
failure correctly in real time.

41:33.880 --> 41:34.380
Exactly.

41:34.680 --> 41:34.920
Okay.

41:35.060 --> 41:39.020
So they have this perfect, pristine mathematical crystal in the shape of a Y.

41:39.160 --> 41:40.600
How do they try to break it?

41:40.600 --> 41:44.420
They developed a framework containing 20 pre-registered mutants.

41:44.560 --> 41:45.020
Mutants.

41:45.300 --> 41:47.020
Like injecting a virus into the code.

41:47.240 --> 41:48.120
Essentially, yes.

41:48.800 --> 41:55.320
A mutant is a deliberate, specific, logical error injected into the code to see if the

41:55.320 --> 41:57.200
Lean4 verification system catches it.

41:57.300 --> 42:00.880
And more importantly, to see how the protocol maps the failure.

42:01.040 --> 42:01.300
Okay.

42:01.740 --> 42:06.700
But here is the most crucial scientific principle of the entire claim shift experiment.

42:06.960 --> 42:10.040
The paper emphasizes this in bold text.

42:10.600 --> 42:13.860
Ground truth first, then observation.

42:14.640 --> 42:15.500
Never the reverse.

42:15.500 --> 42:18.520
So they pre-registered the exact mutants.

42:18.820 --> 42:21.480
Why is it so vitally important that they pre-registered them?

42:21.920 --> 42:26.380
Couldn't they just run the code, mutate it randomly a thousand times, and see what failed?

42:26.640 --> 42:31.540
If you don't predict the exact failure sets beforehand, you fall victim to the trap of

42:31.540 --> 42:32.380
hindsight bias.

42:32.600 --> 42:33.680
Which is a huge issue.

42:34.020 --> 42:36.740
It is a massive persistent problem in software verification.

42:37.140 --> 42:40.680
If you just inject a random error and the system crashes, you might pat yourself on the back and say,

42:40.680 --> 42:42.420
great, the system got the error, it's secure.

42:42.580 --> 42:42.780
Right.

42:43.100 --> 42:44.880
But what if it crashed for the wrong reason?

42:45.140 --> 42:49.420
What if it crashed because of a simple syntax error, like a missing comma, rather than actually

42:49.420 --> 42:52.380
detecting the deep semantic lie you tried to inject?

42:52.580 --> 42:57.840
By pre-registering, Flyxion forced themselves to mathematically predict exactly which theorems

42:57.840 --> 43:03.740
should fail, exactly which edges should break, and exactly which branches the failure should

43:03.740 --> 43:07.440
propagate down, all before the computer ever executed the code.

43:07.500 --> 43:10.280
It's like calling your exact shot in a game of pool.

43:10.540 --> 43:10.980
Exactly.

43:11.120 --> 43:14.680
You have to point and say, eight ball, off the rail, corner pocket.

43:14.940 --> 43:18.940
If you just hit the ball and it happens to go in the side pocket by accident, it doesn't count.

43:19.360 --> 43:21.880
You didn't prove you know how the physics work, you just got lucky.

43:22.200 --> 43:23.160
That's a great way to put it.

43:23.400 --> 43:26.700
You have to prove you understand the exact logical relationship.

43:26.700 --> 43:34.340
Will the mutant make the claim mathematically stronger, weaker, incompatible, or completely

43:34.340 --> 43:35.300
logically refuted?

43:35.480 --> 43:37.760
Okay, let's look at the actual mutants they threw at this thing.

43:38.000 --> 43:41.260
The outline mentions some flagship mutants that really proved the concept.

43:41.680 --> 43:45.880
