43:47.400 --> 43:50.300
Well, let's start with M09, which tests a different mechanic.

43:51.220 --> 43:52.880
M09 is a constant mutation.

43:52.940 --> 43:53.500
What does that do?

43:53.500 --> 43:59.220
They went to the very root of one of the branches, let's call it node D1, and simply changed a

43:59.220 --> 44:00.640
constant numerical value.

44:00.860 --> 44:01.140
Okay.

44:01.840 --> 44:07.400
Based on the type dependency graph, they pre-registered the prediction that this should cause the semantic

44:07.400 --> 44:12.480
theorems on branch one to fail, but the theorems on branch two should remain perfectly valid.

44:12.700 --> 44:13.540
And did it work?

44:13.840 --> 44:16.560
When they ran the lean kernel, that's exactly what happened.

44:16.800 --> 44:19.520
It isolated the branch root propagation perfectly.

44:19.940 --> 44:22.360
The protocol didn't panic and shut down the whole document.

44:22.360 --> 44:22.800
Awesome.

44:22.980 --> 44:23.740
What about M15?

44:24.220 --> 44:25.440
Ah, M15.

44:25.840 --> 44:27.860
This answers your question about deleting a line.

44:28.480 --> 44:31.120
M15 is hypothesis deletion.

44:31.360 --> 44:31.640
Okay.

44:31.820 --> 44:35.340
They systematically deleted a required hypothesis from a central proof.

44:36.200 --> 44:40.720
The fascinating, almost beautiful thing here is that the system didn't just fail passively.

44:41.080 --> 44:44.420
It became actively self-refuting via an existing witness.

44:44.580 --> 44:45.500
Wait, what does that mean?

44:45.500 --> 44:50.620
The kernel-checked code actually contained the mathematical counterexample that proved the

44:50.620 --> 44:53.460
newly mutated theorem was completely universally false.

44:54.000 --> 44:57.480
It actively destroyed the false claim using its own internal logic.

44:57.840 --> 44:58.560
That's incredible.

44:58.680 --> 45:03.520
It's like a mathematical immune system recognizing the virus and immediately producing the exact

45:03.520 --> 45:04.960
antibody to destroy it.

45:05.040 --> 45:05.460
Exactly.

45:05.700 --> 45:06.420
What about M13?

45:06.420 --> 45:12.960
M13 is a quantifier mutation, and the results from this one yield a deeply counterintuitive

45:12.960 --> 45:16.440
insight that AI developers really need to pay attention to.

45:16.600 --> 45:17.400
Okay, I'm listening.

45:17.400 --> 45:23.240
Often, people intuitively assume that if you make a hypothesis weaker, if you make the starting

45:23.240 --> 45:28.880
assumptions less strict or more vague, it should make the downstream proofs easier to

45:28.880 --> 45:29.240
prove.

45:29.460 --> 45:30.940
The bar is lower, right?

45:31.060 --> 45:31.220
Right.

45:31.260 --> 45:36.860
If I say, find me a vehicle instead of, find me a red 2015 Honda Civic, it should be easier

45:36.860 --> 45:37.460
to fulfill.

45:37.780 --> 45:42.960
But M13 proved mathematically that weakening hypotheses can actually make downstream formal

45:42.960 --> 45:45.920
proof harder or even impossible to verify.

45:46.180 --> 45:46.500
Really?

45:46.500 --> 45:47.000
Why?

45:47.400 --> 45:49.680
By making it weaker, you break the interface dependencies.

45:50.180 --> 45:54.660
You no longer have the strict, precise, logical tools you need to finish the complex construction

45:54.660 --> 45:55.260
later on.

45:55.700 --> 45:57.740
The proof collapses because it lacks traction.

45:58.260 --> 46:01.300
So being vaguer doesn't make it easier for the AI to prove it.

46:01.620 --> 46:06.800
It deprives the system of the precise gears it needs to turn to reach the semantic truth.

46:06.960 --> 46:07.340
Exactly.

46:07.340 --> 46:08.620
You lose your grip on the logic.

46:09.220 --> 46:11.160
And finally, we arrive at M12.

46:11.420 --> 46:11.900
M12.

46:12.180 --> 46:13.440
I cannot overstate this.

46:13.440 --> 46:20.320
M12 is perhaps the most important mutant for the entire AI industry to understand right

46:20.320 --> 46:20.540
now.

46:20.700 --> 46:20.860
Okay.

46:20.940 --> 46:21.780
Build the suspense.

46:21.960 --> 46:22.400
What is it?

46:22.580 --> 46:28.040
M12 showed conclusively that checking the parity of a state, meaning just checking the

46:28.040 --> 46:33.900
superficial structural count of variables or nodes in a syntax tree, is way, way too

46:33.900 --> 46:36.340
coarse to detect profound semantic changes.

46:36.340 --> 46:40.600
Let's really break down what checking the parity of a state means, because this feels

46:40.600 --> 46:43.600
like the absolute crux of the issue with AI summarizers.

46:43.820 --> 46:43.880
Okay.

46:44.360 --> 46:49.000
When M12 was injected, it perfectly matched the superficial mathematical invariance of

46:49.000 --> 46:49.800
the original code.

46:49.900 --> 46:50.700
So it looked the same.

46:50.700 --> 46:53.980
If you just counted the variables, if you just looked at the structural shape of the

46:53.980 --> 46:55.980
formula, it looked identical to the original.

46:56.520 --> 46:57.280
The parity matched.

46:57.760 --> 47:01.760
But internally, the logical meaning was completely corrupted and reversed.

47:02.000 --> 47:02.360
Wow.

47:02.580 --> 47:05.000
Let me put this into a visceral real world narrative.

47:05.180 --> 47:11.220
Imagine an AI summarizer is looking at a dense 100 page financial report from a major bank.

47:11.640 --> 47:17.800
The original report has a very specific, deeply verified semantic claim buried on page 40.

47:17.960 --> 47:18.240
Okay.

